title: Why there is no atime or ctime
tags: [[File tracking]]
kind: rationale
summary: Of a file's four timestamps the daemon records mtime and btime: atime moves with the daemon's own reads and the mount options, and ctime only says that something it already records changed — and does not survive a copy.


A file has four timestamps; the watcher and reconcile record two of them
([[File metadata fields]]).

|!Timestamp |!Moves when |!Field |
|mtime (modification) |the content is written |`mfr_mtime` |
|btime (birth) |never: when the inode was created |`mfr_btime`, where the filesystem keeps it |
|ctime (//change//, not creation) |the inode changes: content, mode, owner, a rename, a new hard link |none |
|atime (access) |the file is read |none |

`touch`, `cp -p`, `rsync -t` and archive extractors can set mtime and atime to any
value; ctime cannot be set, and is always "now" at the last change.

!! Not atime

* ''It does not say how the file is used.'' Most Linux filesystems are mounted
  `relatime`: atime moves on a read only once a day, or when it is older than mtime;
  under `noatime` it never moves. Its value depends on the mount options more than
  on the reads.
* ''The daemon would move it itself.'' Fingerprinting ([[File fingerprint]]),
  embedded-metadata extraction ([[Embedded metadata]]), MIME detection and the
  GUI's thumbnails all read files: recording atime would record the daemon's own
  work, and each recorded read would be followed by another.
* ''A read is not a write.'' Every write is an operation in the log
  ([[The operation log]]); one per file read would bury the changes worth undoing
  — the reason recently viewed metarecords are not a field either
  ([[Why recently viewed is kept by the GUI]]).

!! Not ctime

* ''It is redundant.'' Everything that moves it is already recorded, by name: the
  content (`mfr_mtime`, `mfr_size`, the hashes), the mode (`mfr_permissions`), the
  owner (`mfr_uid`, `mfr_gid`), the place (`mfr_path`), the hard links
  (`mfr_inode`). ctime says that one of them changed, not which.
* ''It survives nothing.'' A copy, a backup restored, a file synced from another
  repository gets a ctime of "now". Compared between two sides, it would report
  every copied file as changed — sync compares mtime, size and type instead
  ([[Change detection in sync]]).
* ''The log is a better one.'' Each change of a tracked file's metadata is an
  operation with its time, what changed, and who wrote it — the watcher, a
  command, the trash ([[Operations and their origin]]).
